[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Files, plans and how long a node lives

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[NARRATIVE]
UID: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-FILES-AND-RETENTION
TITLE: Files, plans and how long a node lives
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
WIDGET: rows
STATEMENT: >>>
One node per file; retention keyed on directory; plans, backlogs, beads and ownership.

- One node per file, named for its UID in lowercase, with the document title equal to the node title, in flat plan directories. All 202 names hold; one title mismatches. Applied, but not yet ratified. See [LINK: DEC-NODE-FILE-NAMING]. [OPEN: DEC-NODE-FILE-NAMING]
- How long a node lives is decided by where its file sits, not by its type: plan nodes are collectable (190), spec nodes are durable (8), and nodes beside a package move with that package (4). Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MEMBERSHIP-AND-COLLECTION-CONTRADICT]. [WORKS]
- Whether spec nodes and package-side nodes are one durable class or two ranked ones — and whether architecture may depend on the spec — is unsaid. One plan-to-spec edge exists; no architecture-to-spec edges. [UNADDRESSED]
- No rule says what collects a plan, when, or how a node is promoted out of one. The lean: supersede first, collect later, never delete. See [LINK: DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION] and [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK]. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MEMBERSHIP-AND-COLLECTION-CONTRADICT]. [OPEN: DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN]
- A node belongs to a plan by sitting in its directory. The one-grammar ruling says membership is already a relation — but 112 groomed plan nodes carry no such edge, so there is nothing to remove when a plan dissolves. See [LINK: DEC-ONE-GRAMMAR]. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MEMBERSHIP-AND-COLLECTION-CONTRADICT]. [GAP]
- Settled architecture lives in a directory beside the code it describes, each node linked to a file. That placement retires when a tool can extract the same content from source comments. See [LINK: DEC-ARCH-COLOCATION]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-MECHANISM]. [OPEN: DEC-ARCH-COLOCATION]
- A plan's backlog is one register node; ungroomed items point at it. Grooming raises DEPTH in place and drops that edge; the UID never changes. Who may groom is unstated. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-MECHANISM]. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-IDENTITY-HAS-NO-CARRIER]. [WORKS]
- Beads is the agent's private working store, one per identity. Canon and beads differ in authority, not shape. From the moment a plan is handed over, execution state — done, who holds it, what blocks it — lives in beads. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK]. [WORKS]
- One human owns a plan (though it is recorded nowhere); work can involve many identities; handover freezes the scope handed over, not the whole plan; and an agent may file sketch nodes without asking. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK]. [WORKS]
- An agent's key is a second writer on every plan, but the difference between an owner and a delegate is unwritten. The retirement condition 'two people edit one plan' can be read more than one way, and nothing says which. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-IDENTITY-HAS-NO-CARRIER]. [GAP]
<<<
